<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=magnetic-tape">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magnetic tape</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magnetic tape is a storage medium made of a thin magnetizable coating on a long, narrow strip of plastic film. It was developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1928, based on earlier magnetic wire recording from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and Fritz Pfleumer invented it for recording sound. Because of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the German work was kept largely secret, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> learned of it only when they captured German recording equipment at the end of the war. Americans including Jack Mullin, John Herbert Orr and Richard H. Ranger then developed it into commercial formats, and Bing Crosby invested in the manufacturer Ampex. From the early 1950s it was also used to store computer data.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magnetic-tape.jpg" alt="Magnetic tape" width="330" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel P. B. Smith. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magtape1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
